    "description": "Branch management systems support the local execution and supervision of banking operations. Iranian product descriptions illustrate functions including cash control, access permissions, branch reporting, and assisted self-service.",
    "tags": ["Branch banking", "Cash management", "Banking operations", "Iran", "Self-service banking"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T18:25:36Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T18:25:36Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Branch Management Systems in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore branch cash control, reporting, permissions, and assisted self-service through documented Iranian banking products."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "local-operations",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "The branch remains an operating unit",
        "content": "A branch-management function does not necessarily require a standalone product. Pooya’s account-management system, for example, includes branch cash management within a broader banking platform. The supplier describes monitoring cash volumes against bank-defined rules, differentiated user access, branch balance sheets, and supervisory reporting. These functions concern local operations and accountability even when the underlying information is centralized. [s1](https://pooyaco.com/website/?page_id=1057&lang=en)\n\nFor readers, this separates two questions: where information is maintained, and which organizational unit performs or supervises an activity. A shared platform can support branch-level responsibilities. The available documentation establishes advertised functions, but does not measure their effectiveness in preventing errors or controlling cash. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "accounting-visibility",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Local activity must remain visible",
        "content": "Pooya’s financial-management documentation describes centrally stored information from geographically dispersed units while retaining organizational hierarchy and unit-specific permissions. It also lists management of resource surpluses and shortages across regional branches. These features illustrate how branch operations can contribute to a wider financial view. [s2](https://pooyaco.com/website/?page_id=1064&lang=en)\n\nCentral reporting and local responsibility are therefore related design concerns. Their practical effectiveness would require evidence from an implementing bank, including how exceptions are resolved and responsibilities assigned. The supplier page alone cannot establish those outcomes. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "assisted-self-service",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Service points can combine machines and human assistance",
        "content": "Branch technology also extends beyond the teller workstation. Pooya describes a modular self-service branch comprising different service stands. Its virtual teller machine, or VTM, connects a customer to a remote banking service centre through live audio and video. Advertised functions include assistance with account opening and cheque operations. [s3](https://pooyaco.com/website/?page_id=1072&lang=en)\n\nThis is a useful distinction between unattended transaction equipment and remotely assisted service. A physical service point may connect the customer to staff elsewhere. The product description establishes an offered model, not nationwide availability, legal eligibility for every customer, or permission for every advertised operation. Its claimed cost and convenience benefits have not been independently verified here. [s3]\n\nA fuller account of branch management in Iran needs bank-level evidence showing which tasks remain local, which are centralized, and how service arrangements differ between locations.",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      }
    ],
